      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Perceptions of cultural displacement can translate into anxieties about neighborhood stability, even if objective measures of crime don't change significantly. Original residents might associate cultural shifts with a dilution of familiar social capital—the informal networks and trust that defined the community. If the visible cultural landscape changes rapidly, it can foster suspicion, leading some established groups to perceive a decline in \"safety\" not because of crime, but because the familiar social contract underpinning trust has been fractured. This breakdown erodes the traditional, shared understanding of what makes the neighborhood \"home.\"",
